  "step_3": "The decision reason relies on assessing and comparing the skewness of the two lotteries, favoring the one with more probability mass on favorable outcomes. The individual in the verbal report considers the same underlying information—outcome magnitudes and probabilities—and notes that one lottery has a much higher chance of a positive outcome. This aligns with the informational basis of the skewness reason. However, the justification is framed in terms of a trade-off between reward size and win probability, not in terms of the shape or skew of the distribution. While the conclusion (favoring high probability of gain) is consistent with what the skewness reason would suggest, the reasoning process described is more aligned with a 'maximize chance of winning' heuristic than a formal or conceptual use of skewness. There is no indication that the individual evaluated the distribution's asymmetry as a rule-based criterion. Therefore, while the input information overlaps, the reasoning rule does not clearly match the skewness reason. The similarity in outcome consideration suggests partial alignment, but the absence of any structural or distributional language reduces confidence that the skewness reason was actively used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 40
